<%# Today's movers, replacing the scrolling ticker that was in the app-side header.

    **Why this exists and why the ticker went** - the long version is in design.md and migration.md.
    Short: a ticker is a broadcast device for a captive viewer who cannot scroll, it failed WCAG 2.2.2 at
    Level A with no pause control, its two colours measured 2.74:1 and 3.78:1, and it showed a symbol and a
    percentage with no price, no name and no link. This is the same idea in the form an application uses.

    **The help text is not decoration.** A "biggest movers" list put in front of eleven-year-olds teaches
    performance chasing - buy what went up - which is the opposite of what this app is for. So the subtitle
    says what the list is, that moving most does not make a company a better buy, and what to do instead.
    If the card cannot carry that sentence, it should not carry the list either.

    No investment advice, and none is implied: the instruction is to go and read what the company does,
    which is the thing a student can actually act on.

    **It is the subtitle, not a footer band, and that took two goes.** First it was one `<p class="max-w-2xl">`
    in a footer under the rows - 672px inside a 1081px card, so 407px of the card sat empty and the note read
    as truncated. Then it was two columns, which used the width but was reported as jarring across
    viewports, and fairly: a band that is two columns at `lg` and one below it reflows into a different
    shape at every width, under rows that never change shape at all.

    A card's supporting line belongs under its title, where every card in this app puts it, and where it is
    read *before* the list rather than after it - which is the right order for a caution. Condensing it to
    one sentence is what made that possible, and the sentence is better for being shorter.

    Rows are list rows in one card with `divide-y`, not three cards - design.md's card-soup rule. Each row
    is the link, so the target is the row rather than a word inside it. %>
<% movers = Stock.movers %>

<%# 121 characters, which is one line from about 1090px up and two below it. The first draft was 155 and
    broke to two lines at a Chromebook while staying one at 1400 - the same sentence changing shape between
    the two widths this app is actually used at. Shorter is also better copy.

    Held in a local because an ERB comment cannot go inside the `locals:` hash: the `<%%= render %>` tag
    would end at the comment's own terminator. erb_lint passes it and the page returns a 500. %>
<% subtitle = "The biggest price changes since yesterday. A big move does not make a company a better " \
              "buy - open one to see what it does." %>

<%= render layout: "components/ui/card",
           locals: { title: "Today's movers",
                     subtitle: subtitle,
                     icon: "trending-up",
                     icon_tone: :info,
                     padded: movers.none? } do %>
  <% if movers.any? %>
    <ul class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
      <% movers.each do |stock| %>
        <li>
          <%= link_to stock_path(stock),
                      class: "flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-3 transition-colors " \
                             "hover:bg-slate-50 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 " \
                             "focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary" do %>
            <span class="min-w-0">
              <span class="block truncate text-sm font-medium text-slate-900"><%= stock.company_name %></span>
              <span class="block text-xs text-slate-600"><%= stock.ticker.upcase %></span>
            </span>

            <span class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-4">
              <span class="text-sm tabular-nums text-slate-900">
                <%= number_to_currency(stock.current_price) %>
              </span>

              <%# Three states, because two is wrong: the ticker used `percentage_change >= 0`, so a stock
                  that had not moved was green with an upward arrow. `movers` cannot return an unchanged
                  stock, so the neutral branch is unreachable from here - it stays because the branch is
                  the rule, and a caller that shows a named stock rather than a mover will need it.

                  green-700 and red-700, measured 4.95:1 and 6.42:1 on white, against the ticker's 2.74:1
                  and 3.78:1. The sign and the arrow carry the direction too, so it is never colour alone. %>
              <span class="flex w-24 shrink-0 items-center justify-end gap-1 text-sm tabular-nums <%= movement_class(stock.percentage_change) %>">
                <% if stock.percentage_change.positive? %>
                  <%= lucide_icon("trending-up", class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>
                <% elsif stock.percentage_change.negative? %>
                  <%= lucide_icon("trending-down", class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>
                <% end %>
                <%= stock.percentage_change_formatted %>
              </span>
            </span>
          <% end %>
        </li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>

  <% else %>
    <%= render layout: "components/ui/empty_state",
               locals: { compact: true,
                         title: "No price movement recorded yet",
                         body: "Prices are checked once a day. Changes show up here from the second day " \
                               "onwards." } do %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
